+The manager stops a host when its app is disabled, revoked, changed or removed, when its Stop is pressed in the Processes view (behind a confirmation naming the app) and when the server closes: `oculus/deactivate` is sent, the host has `apps.host_stop_seconds` to settle, then SIGTERM follows, then SIGKILL 2 seconds later. The close of the server closes its context, which stops its hosts the same way. Two activation events at once start one process, and an activation during a stop starts one host after the old one has exited. A host that ends on its own is not started again: each open tab of the app shows its exit code or signal with a Restart button, and the Processes view shows the same row. The host's standard error is kept as its last 1000 lines and at most `apps.host_output_kb` kilobytes, and reaches the `app-hosts` channel, which no app reads. A request in flight when its host stops rejects `host_down`.
+
+**Activation.** The host starts at the first of the app's `activation` events. `onTab`: a tab of the app is drawn, and a `host.request` of a tab always starts a host that is not running. `onStartupFinished`: the server has read its apps after it started, or the app becomes approved and enabled. `workspaceContains:<glob>`: a file matching the glob (picomatch, relative to a root) exists in a root the file browser shows, the app's own root for a root app and every shown root for a machine app; `host/triggers.ts` looks when the roots or the app change, reads at most `apps.scan_entries` entries per root, enters no dot-led folder and no `node_modules`, follows no link and matches only paths inside the root. `onLanguage:<id>`: a source tab of a file of that language id opens on a page. `onCommand:<id>` and `onView:<id>` are fired by the manager's one activation call, which the steps that add commands and views call; until they exist these two never fire, and the manifest accepts them.
+
+**Dependencies.** Before a host starts, the host of each app in its `depends` starts. What a dependency's `activate` returns is its API: `oculus.apps.get(name)` answers an object whose functions call that API through `oculus/apps/get` and `oculus/apps/call` with JSON arguments and results. A name not in `depends` rejects `not_declared`, a dependency that is not running and cannot start `host_down`. Disable or Remove of an app that enabled apps depend on asks first, naming those apps, and on yes disables them too and stops their hosts, the dependency's host last. A revoke of a dependency stops the depending hosts and each tab and Processes row says whose revoke stopped it. A dependency that is missing, refused, disabled or unapproved leaves the depending host waiting and named; a cycle of `depends` starts none of its apps and the Processes view says so.
+
+**The protocol.** Every message between oculus and a host is JSON-RPC 2.0 over the host's standard input and output through `vscode-jsonrpc`'s `createMessageConnection` on both ends, with requests and notifications both ways, cancellation by `$/cancelRequest` and a cancellation token for the handler, and progress by `$/progress`. The method names are internal to oculus and its runner. This table holds every method, those the apps' host builds and the ones later steps add; a later step adds rows and changes none.
+
+| Method | Direction | Kind | Carries | Built |
+|--------|-----------|------|---------|-------|
+| `oculus/activate` | to the host | request | the activation event, the app's recorded files as real paths and the runner's sentences | yes |
+| `oculus/request` | to the host | request | a page's `host.request`: method, params and the asking tab | yes |
+| `oculus/storeChanged` | to the host | notification | the keys that changed in the store | yes |
+| `oculus/apiCall` | to the host | request | a dependent's call of this host's API: the function and its JSON arguments, or no function to list them | yes |
+| `oculus/deactivate` | to the host | request | nothing | yes |
+| `oculus/store/get`, `oculus/store/update`, `oculus/store/keys` | from the host | request | a key, a key and a value, nothing | yes |
+| `oculus/secrets/get`, `oculus/secrets/store`, `oculus/secrets/delete` | from the host | request | a key, a key and a value, a key | yes |
+| `oculus/sendToPage` | from the host | request | a method, params and a tab, or none for every open tab | yes |
+| `oculus/apps/get`, `oculus/apps/call` | from the host | request | a dependency's name; its name, a function and arguments | yes |
+| `oculus/register`, `oculus/unregister` | from the host | request | a contribution by kind, id and options | later step |
+| `oculus/provide` | to the host | request | oculus asks a registered provider for its answer | later step |
+| `oculus/event` | to the host | notification | an event the host subscribed to or a change of a setting | later step |
+| `oculus/subscribe`, `oculus/unsubscribe` | from the host | request | the events of one kind | later step |
+| `oculus/ui` | from the host | request | a quick pick, an input box, a message or a progress | later step |
+| `oculus/stream/open`, `oculus/stream/data`, `oculus/stream/control`, `oculus/stream/close` | both ways | request, notification | a byte or message stream with an id, for a language server, a debug adapter or a terminal a host runs; control carries a terminal's rows and columns or an end signal | later step |
+| `oculus/changed` | from the host | notification | a registered contribution changed: a status item's text, a tree's rows, source control groups, file decorations; a second `register` of the same id is refused | later step |
+| `oculus/settings/get` | from the host | request | one of the host's declared settings | later step |
+| `oculus/commands/execute` | from the host | request | a command by id, oculus's own or an app's | later step |
+
+The page's side has a second table. The frame methods built with the host are `fs.readFile`, `fs.readDirectory`, `fs.stat`, `commands.run`, `secrets.get`, `secrets.store`, `secrets.delete` and `host.request`, and the handler `onHost(method, params)`. The methods and handler that later steps add are `ui.quickPick`, `ui.inputBox`, `ui.message`, `ui.progress`, `register` and `unregister` of a contribution a frame drives, `document.read` and `document.save` for a custom editor's document, and the handler `onVisibility(visible)` of a view's frame.
+
+**Frame methods of the host.** `oculus.fs.readFile({ root, path })` answers a `Uint8Array`, `readDirectory({ root, path })` a list of `[name, type]` and `stat({ root, path })` `{ type, ctime, mtime, size }`, `type` the editor's `FileType` (1 file, 2 folder, 64 added for a link). For a machine app the folder must lie in a `files` entry, else `not_declared`; for a root app it is its own root. The checks of `open({ file })` follow in their order (`reach/open.ts` `checkOpen`), then `reach/fs.ts` answers from the server to the frame over the app's own address: a route under `/apps/<name>/<key>/` (`/apps/@<root>/<name>/<key>/` for a root app) on a dot-led first segment (`.fs`, `.dir`, `.stat`) that no app file can have, answered only under the app's current key while the app is approved and enabled, as `application/octet-stream` with `nosniff`, `Content-Disposition: attachment` and the app's sandbox policy, so an `.html` or `.svg` file of a root is never drawn as a document on oculus's origin. The body is a stream of the file read in chunks, so the page never holds the bytes and no size limit applies. A refusal is a plain-text word with a status of 403 for `closed` and `not_declared` and 404 for `missing`; a wrong key, an unapproved or disabled app or a dot-led segment before the root key is the empty 404. A FIFO, socket or device file answers `closed` without being opened. `readDirectory` lists no dot-led name and no name `closed_paths` closes, and at most `apps.scan_entries` names. `commands.run(name)` runs the command of that name from the app's root's `.oculus/commands.yml` (the hub's for a machine app) through `runCommand` and its chain approval, in a terminal the reader sees; it resolves once the terminal is open, rejects `missing` for no such command and `declined` when the reader refuses, and counts under `apps.opens_per_min`. A command saved through the form is not reachable. `oculus.host.request(method, params)` sends to the host with the tab's id and answers what the handler answers: `not_declared` for an app with no `host`, `host_down` when the host is not running and cannot start, `refused:<message>` for a handler that threw. `connect` takes a fourth handler, `onHost(method, params)`, called for each `sendToPage` to the tab; the page routes a message of the `app-messages` channel to the frames of its app, to the tab it names or to every tab (`hostTargets` in `state/system/apps/rules.ts`). `penpal` carries the calls under flat names that `oculus.js` nests.
+
+**The store from the host.** The connection object's `store` is the app's one store file: the map the frame's `getStore` and `setStore` read and write whole. `update(key, value)` changes one key and keeps every other byte of the value it read, a value of the file that is not a map is replaced by a map holding the one key, and `undefined` deletes the key. Each `update` is a store write: under `apps.store_max_kb`, counted under `apps.writes_per_min` per host, recorded in the change log and undone from History as a frame's `setStore` is, and announced to every tab's `onStore`. A change another writer makes reaches the host as `oculus/storeChanged` with the keys that changed, and a change the host made itself is not told back. The store writes of one app are serialised through one queue per store file, the frame's `setStore` and the host's `update` alike, so two at the same moment both land and no tab receives a store value older than one it has seen.
+
+**Secrets.** `@napi-rs/keyring` 2.1.0, pinned in `package.json`, is the one code path on every platform and in the browser mode (`secrets/secrets.ts`; the module is passed in, so a test hands in a store that keeps entries in memory). The service is `oculus-app:<view id>` and the account is the key, 1 to 64 of `[A-Za-z0-9._-]`, else `refused:key`; a value is UTF-8 and at most 2048 bytes, else `too_large`. On Linux the entry is made with `{ linux: { store: 'secret-service' } }`, so a machine with no Secret Service, or no store at all, refuses with a line that says so, for `get` and `delete` as for `store`. `get` of an absent key answers `undefined`. An app without `secrets: true` is refused `not_declared` by every secret method. The frame's `oculus.secrets` reaches the server through `POST /api/app-secrets` with the write token and the `x-oculus-app` header, as a write does, and for its own app alone; the host's reaches the same module over the protocol. A store or delete counts under `apps.writes_per_min`, is not recorded in the change log, and no secret value is logged, published, put in a channel or written to any file. Remove counts an app's secrets with `findCredentials(service)` and drops the values at once.
+
+**Enable and disable.** An app's enable state is kept in `~/.oculus/apps.json`, mode 0600, written through a temporary file and a rename with its writes chained so two at once both land (`manage/enable.ts`): `{ "version": 1, "disabled": [<view id>], "workspaces": { "<workspace name, or default>": { "disabled": [<view id>], "enabled": [<view id>] } } }`. An app is disabled in the current workspace when its view id is in that workspace's `disabled`, or in the machine-wide `disabled` and not in that workspace's `enabled`. Disable and Enable write the machine-wide list; Disable (Workspace) and Enable (Workspace) write the current workspace's lists and keep the two lists of one workspace apart. The current workspace is the one `/api/fs` answers as current, `default` for the default workspace; a saved workspace is never renamed or deleted, so no entry is carried, and a switch of workspace starts and stops hosts as the new state says. A file that does not read is never overwritten; one that does not parse reads every app as disabled, the finding `state_unread` names it, and Enable and Disable are refused with that reason until it is put right. An entry for a view id no app has is kept, so an app removed from a root and put back keeps its state, and Remove drops the removed app's entries. A disabled app stays in the catalogue with a disabled mark (`app_disabled`, `apps.disabled_mark`), its tabs show `apps.disabled_line` with an Enable button in place of the frame, its host stops, and the server refuses its file route, its fs route, its secrets, its host requests, its writes through the gate and its store. `POST /api/app-manage` carries `disable`, `enable`, `inspect` (what a removal will do) and `remove`, each with the write token and behind the write chain; a disable or removal that enabled apps depend on answers 409 with their titles until it is sent again with `cascade`.
+
+**Remove and add.** Remove is on a machine app's right-click menu and approval panel. The question names the folder and the number of secrets the app holds; on yes the host stops, the folder and its store file go to the system trash through the `trash` package (a link in the folder moves as a link and never its target), every secret of the app is deleted from the keyring, its approval is revoked and its disabled state dropped. With the keyring unavailable Remove goes ahead and its question says the secrets could not be counted and will not be deleted. A root app offers no Remove. Add app from folder is an action of the catalogue's Apps group, with the folder chosen in oculus's folder panel, and Add as App on the right-click menu of a Files view folder that holds an `app.yml`. `POST /api/app-add` checks, before a byte is copied, that the folder holds `app.yml`, has a name matching the name pattern that no entry of the apps folder holds (an app, a refused folder or any file), lies neither inside the apps folder nor holds it, and fits `apps.max_files` and `apps.max_mb`, following no link loop. It copies the folder without links and dot-led names, as the approval hash leaves them out, into a temporary folder beside the apps folder and renames it into place, so no partial app folder is ever read; the copied app appears unapproved and asks as any app does. A refusal says why in one line and copies nothing, and where the folder panel answers `unsupported` the action says so and names Add as App.
+
+**Words.** The words the reader sees come from `config/text/` through `t("group.key")`, in one file, `config/text/views/apps/apps.yml`, holding the group `apps`: `label`, `glyph`, `unapproved_mark`, `editing_mark`, `approve_title`, `approve_question`, `approve`, `keep_approved`, `revoke`, `open_folder`, `reads_none`, `writes_none`, `connects`, `connects_none`, `store_machine`, `store_root`, `read.<channel>` for each channel a machine app may declare (`portfolio`, `feeds`, `git`, `hpc`, `audit`, `config`, `files`, `inbox`, `calendar`, `sessions` and `usage`), `read_root.git`, `read_root.files`, `write.<kind>` for each kind a manifest may declare, `ask.<kind>` for each kind that asks first (the verb phrase `confirm_write` puts after "asks to"), `changed`, `load_failed`, `refused_read`, `refused_write`, `refused_open`, `confirm_write`, `confirm_open_url`, `rate`, the sentences of the host (`host_line`, `files_line`, `secrets_line`, `depends_line`, `send_line`, `host_ended_code`, `host_ended_signal`, `host_wait_approval`, `host_wait_dependency`, `host_wait_cycle`, `host_stopped_by`, `restart`, `review`) and the sentences of managing apps (`disabled_mark`, `disabled_line`, `enable`, `menu_*`, `disable_dependents`, `remove_*`, `add_*`, `manage_failed`). The finding sentences are in `config/text/findings.yml` under `findings.apps` with the keys named above, and `findings.apps.carries.<channel>` for `apps`, `pty`, `processes`, `inbox`, `calendar`, `sessions` and `usage`. An app whose bridge handshake does not complete within `apps.connect_timeout_s` (10), the timeout given to `connect`, draws `apps.load_failed` over the frame with a control that reloads it; an app that reads and writes nothing still calls `connect`.
+
+The keys of an `apps:` block in `config/behavior.yml` are the limits `connect_timeout_s`, `state_max_kb`, `store_max_kb`, `writes_per_min`, `opens_per_min`, `messages_per_min` (600), `keys_per_s`, `max_files`, `max_mb`, `host_output_kb` (256), `host_stop_seconds` (5) and `scan_entries` (10000), and the list `closed_paths` (`[secrets]` in the file), read by `appsSettings`, which sits beside `mailSettings` in `src/lib/data/sources/config/settings.ts` and requires the limits as `mailSettings` requires its keys; `closed_paths` alone is reported as a finding, with files closed, as above.
 
